; Tutorial_3_Convert_PDFs_to_Markdown.inix -- a spoken walk through BatCon, written for buildTutorials (the format is
; described at the top of Tutorial_0_Overview.inix in the Homer kit's Templates).

[about]
Title=3 - Convert PDFs to Markdown
Intro=A folder of PDF files, each made a Markdown file you can read line by line.
Setup=BatCon is open, and a folder holds a few PDF files.
Homework=Convert a folder of your own PDFs.

[step]
Say=Markdown is plain text with a little structure: headings start with a number sign, and list items with a dash. A screen reader reads it line by line, and its headings make a long document easy to move through.

[step]
Say=Start in Source paths. Alt plus S, Sierra, goes there from anywhere in the dialog.
Key=Alt+S
Hear=Source paths: edit, Documents

[step]
Say=Type the folder and a pattern together, here C colon backslash Reports backslash star dot P D F. The star stands for any name, so this means every PDF in Reports.
Note=C:\Reports\*.pdf

[step]
Say=Tab away from the field. Output directory follows what you typed, and now names the Reports folder.
Key=Tab
Hear=Browse local source button

[step]
Say=Alt plus O, Oscar, to hear it.
Key=Alt+O
Hear=Output directory: edit, C colon backslash Reports

[step]
Say=Alt plus T, Tango, for Target format. Markdown document may already be chosen; if not, press M to jump to it.
Key=Alt+T
Key=M
Hear=Markdown document (md)

[step]
Say=A tagged PDF, made with care for accessibility, keeps its headings and lists. An untagged one is read by its layout: large bold lines become headings, and columns are put in reading order.

[step]
Say=A scanned PDF holds pictures of pages rather than text. BatCon reads those by recognizing the letters, which takes longer, and needs the Tesseract tool installed.

[step]
Say=Press Enter to start. BatCon says how many files it is converting, then names each as it goes, no more often than every two seconds, so a long batch is never silent.
Key=Enter
Hear=Converting 3 files to md
Hear=2 of 3: Budget dot p d f

[step]
Say=When it is done, the Results box opens. Its first line is the summary.
Hear=BatCon results
Hear=3 files converted, 0 kept as they were, 0 skipped for want of a conversion, 0 failed.

[step]
Say=The reader goes on to each file's line, saying what it became.
Hear=Converted Budget dot p d f to Budget dot m d.

[step]
Say=Escape closes the Results box. The Markdown files are now beside the PDFs, with the same names and the extension dot m d.
Key=Escape
Note=The reader says nothing as the window closes.

[step]
Say=A folder means the files in it, not the folders inside it. To take in PDFs from another folder as well, add it to Source paths, with a space between the two.

[step]
Say=A web page is the other good target for reading. Pick Web page in Target format, Alt plus T and the letter W, and each PDF becomes a page with the same headings, which a browser's heading keys move through.
Key=Alt+T
Key=W
Hear=Web page (htm)

[step]
Say=Word document is a third choice, when someone wants to edit the text: after Web page, the letter W once more reaches it.

[step]
Say=What this walk taught: a pattern in Source paths, Markdown document in Target format, and Enter; and that scanned pages are read by recognizing the letters.
